The core idea is this. Work on your feature and use as many changesets as needed. When you are ready to give it up for review (or periodically before you rebase with ToT)
 * hg histedit and collapse all your change into 1
  So what you did is collapsed your phases into a single 1

 * Now break it apart into as many logical changes (ideally based on files) that would make reviewing this change easy
  use hg split for doing this. It prompts you which diffs should go into what changeset

 * Alright, at this point your changeset history is review friendly but not reflective of how you developed them (that's ok)

 * As you get more updates on your reviews, hg up changeset, make the change and then do a hg amend
  Then use hg rebase -s newer_changelist_parent_of_old_changelist -d newly_added_changelist_which_is_cow_of_the_original_changelist

  hg up tip

That's it. Your changeset history will continue to reflect the reviews and going to top, you can have all the changes in one place

